## 11. Known Issues & Notes

**Relationship casing**: Two relationship names use lowercase "was" instead of "Was":
`"Victim was Ex-Spouse"` and `"Victim was Foster Parent"`. Both forms are hard-coded
in every IN filter above. If the source data is corrected, update each measure.

**NULL relationship rows**: `victim_count` and `offender_count` rows have a NULL
`relationship`. When `_RelKey = "INTIMATE_PARTNER"` or `"FAMILY"`, these rows are
excluded by the IN filter, which is correct — they are aggregate count rows, not
offense-specific rows.

**Offender demographic + Victim slicer mismatch**: `offender_count` rows carry
offender demographics (`off_sex`, `off_race`, `off_age`) but null victim demographics.
If the demographic slicer is set to a victim dimension and the offense slicer to
`offender_count`, `[Crime_Count]` will return 0 for all demographic axis values.
Recommend either: (a) exclude `offender_count` from the offense slicer default, or
(b) add a note to the page explaining this combination.

**county_2**: Multi-county agencies produce rows with a second county in `county_2`.
These incidents are attributed to the primary `county` only in all measures above.
`county_2` is present in the fact table if future attribution to both counties is
needed.

**victim_count vs offense metrics**: `victim_count` counts unique DV victims;
offense-specific metrics count victim–offense pairs and will exceed `victim_count`
when one victim has multiple offenses in the same incident. Do not add offense-type
counts to `victim_count` in the same visual.
